# Application

`AppContext` is the smallest shared composition root. It owns explicit modules,
typed bindings, and reverse-order disposal.

## Install only product capabilities

```dart
final context = await AppContext.create(<Module>[
  QueryClientModule(),
]);

final query = context.read(queryClientKey);
await context.dispose();
```

Module ownership transfers as each module is yielded to `AppContext.create`.
If enumeration, registration, or initialization fails, every yielded module is
disposed in reverse order. Modules must therefore tolerate setup that never
started or only partly completed. Concurrent and repeated `dispose()` calls
join the same future. Public disposal during setup is rejected so initialization
cannot resume on a destroyed context. Cleanup may still read already-created
context values, but cannot first materialize a lazy factory after its owner has
released. After cleanup settles, new reads fail closed.

A binding must have a typed `ContextKey`. There is no string service locator
and no implicit global registry.

## Identity keys

```dart
final sessionKey = ContextKey<Session>('session');

sessionKey.provide(registry, session);
final current = context.read(sessionKey);
```

A key is matched by instance identity, not by its diagnostic name. Define one
top-level `final` key and reuse that exact instance when providing and reading
the value. Independent modules may use the same natural name without
overwriting each other.

This replaces the old `const ContextKey(...)` form. Do not reconstruct a key
at a read site: a new instance intentionally names a different binding.
Registration is key-first so the analyzer fixes the value type from the key
instead of widening an incompatible key/value pair to `Object`. Lazy values
use `sessionKey.provideFactory(registry, createSession)`.

## Flutter composition

`App` creates one application context and exposes it to a builder.

```dart
runApp(
  App(
    modules: <Module>[
      QueryModule(),
      RouterModule(routes: routeTree),
    ],
    builder: (app) => Product(router: app.read(routerKey)),
  ),
);
```

`App` preserves the same lazy-enumeration rollback contract. Initialization
failures go to `errorBuilder`; secondary rollback and later disposal failures
are reported once through `FlutterError`.

## Request scope

The server creates a fresh application context for each request. Request
modules may borrow a process-owned database with
`DatabaseModule.borrowed(database)`, then read it through `databaseKey`.
The `Server` `onClose` callback owns the one process-level `database.close`
call. Never put
`DatabaseModule.owned(sharedDatabase)` in `Server.modules`: the first completed
request would close the shared pool.

Request modules are disposed only after the response body, nested
`waitUntil` work, and cancellation cleanup settle. `Server.close()` first
rejects new invocations, drains those request lifetimes, then runs `onClose`.
